<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kogi people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kogi are an Indigenous people of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-nevada-de-santa-marta">Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta</a> in Colombia, speaking a Chibchan language and widely regarded as heirs of the Tairona. Led by spiritual authorities called mamos, they see themselves as guardians of the balance of the world. Their traditional life and warnings about environmental destruction became widely known through the 1990 BBC documentary From the Heart of the World.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kogi-people.jpg" alt="Kogi people" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mäůřıçîö Bolaño · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hombrekogui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kogi call themselves Kágaba, a name meaning jaguar in their language, which belongs to the Chibchan family and is close to the languages of the Arhuaco and Ika of the same mountains. They descend from the Tairona culture, which built stone structures and paths in the forest and made gold objects. Around 1000 CE, before the Spanish arrived, invading Caribs drove the Tairona into the highlands, a refuge that later proved strategic. The view, associated above all with Gerardo Reichel-Dolmatoff, that their culture has continued unbroken since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pre-columbian-era">Pre-Columbian era</a> has been criticised for passing over centuries of contact and for picturing the Kogi as noble eco-savages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Missionaries later built chapels and churches among their villages, but life in the mountains spared the Kogi the worst effects of colonisation. They keep outsiders out of their ancestral lands, and only men deal with strangers. To warn the outside world, whom they call Younger Brother, of ecological ruin, the mamos let a small BBC crew film them for the 1990 documentary, then withdrew again. Twenty years on they called on the same film-maker, Alan Ereira, and in the 2012 film Aluna they gave a second warning, linking reckless change to ecosystems and the desecration of tombs with climate change.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Kogi belief rests on Aluna, the thought of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Great Mother</a>, who created the world from images in her mind; the Earth is a living being and humanity its children. The Kogi see themselves as Elder Brothers guarding the Heart of the World, the peak of Gonawindua, also called Pico Cristóbal Colón. Mamos, priests trained from early childhood in isolation through abstinence from food, sleep and sex, bless seeds and marriages and make offerings to keep the world in balance.</p><p>Villages of round huts of stone, mud and palm leaves each have a men's temple, the nuhue, where decisions are taken. Everyone wears white, the colour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Great Mother</a>. On coming of age a man receives a poporo, a gourd of lime powder taken with chewed coca, and men greet one another by exchanging coca. Families farm plots at several altitudes, raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> on the highlands and trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kogi people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their descendants, the <b>Kogi</b>, Arhuaco, Wiwa and Kankuamo peoples, still live in the Sierra.</blockquote><a href="/state/tairona">Tairona</a></li></ul></section>
